In the realm of gauge theory, curvature does not mean a line bending on a piece of paper.

14:21.420 --> 14:26.220
Curvature measures the infinitesimal failure of continuations to commute along nearby paths.

14:26.300 --> 14:27.260
Wait, failure to commute?

14:27.420 --> 14:30.400
We need to define commute in this context for everyone listening,

14:30.600 --> 14:33.860
because we are definitely not talking about sitting in traffic on the way to the office.

14:34.200 --> 14:34.840
Right, no.

14:34.840 --> 14:42.240
In mathematics, two operations are said to commute if changing the order in which you perform them does not change the final result.

14:42.720 --> 14:44.520
The classic example is basic addition.

14:45.080 --> 14:46.660
Two plus three equals five.

14:47.360 --> 14:49.880
And three plus two also equals five.

14:50.440 --> 14:51.860
The operations commute.

14:52.840 --> 14:54.140
The order is irrelevant.

14:54.900 --> 14:57.800
But in many systems, operations do not commute.

14:58.220 --> 14:59.740
The order matters fundamentally.

